                <p>The Protein Language Model project focuses on learning the underlying “language” of proteins by treating amino acid sequences as structured text. Using large-scale pretraining on the UniRef90 dataset, the model applies BPE tokenization and a Transformer-based architecture (similar to BERT) to capture contextual and evolutionary dependencies among residues. By learning rich sequence representations, the pretrained model serves as a foundation for diverse downstream biological tasks.
These downstream tasks span multiple dimensions of protein understanding:
Protein Structure Prediction – including secondary structure classification, disorder region detection, remote homology recognition, and fold class prediction.
Post-Translational Modifications – addressing signal peptide identification, major PTM recognition, and neuropeptide cleavage site prediction.
Biophysical Properties – modeling protein fluorescence and stability to support rational protein design.
Inverse Protein Folding – generating amino acid sequences that align with desired 3D structures, enabling structure-conditioned protein design.
By unifying these tasks under a shared pretrained framework, the Protein Language Model aims to establish a comprehensive foundation for protein sequence understanding, function prediction, and design — bridging the gap between bioinformatics and artificial intelligence.</p>
